WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
So what does deep learning have to do with the brain? At the risk of giving away the punch line,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.240
I would say not a whole lot. But let's take a quick look at why people keep making the analogy

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.080
between deep learning and the human brain. When you implement a neural network, this is what you do,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.160
board prop and back prop. And I think because it's been difficult to convey intuitions about what

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:28.160
these equations are doing, really gradient descent on a very complex function, the analogy that is

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:34.320
like the brain has become really an oversimplified explanation for what this is doing. But the

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.560
simplicity of this makes it kind of seductive for people to just say it publicly, as well as for

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.080
media to report it. And the survey called the popular imagination. And there is a very loose

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:53.600
analogy between, let's say, a logistic regression unit with a sigmoid activation function. And

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.160
here's a cartoon of a single neuron in the brain. In this picture of a biological neuron,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.200
this neuron, which is a cell in your brain, receives electric signals from other neurons,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.640
x1, x2, x3, or maybe from other neurons, a1, a2, a3. There's a simple,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.720
thresholded computation. And then if this neuron fires, it sends a pulse of electricity

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:26.240
down the axon, down this long wire, perhaps to other neurons. So there is a very simplistic

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.040
analogy between a single logistic unit, between a single neuron in the neural network,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:37.360
and a biological neuron like that shown on the right. But I think that today, even neuroscientists

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.280
have almost no idea what even a single neuron is doing. A single neuron appears to be much more

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:49.920
complex than we are able to characterize with neuroscience. And while some of what it's doing

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.760
is a little bit like logistic regression, there's still a lot about what even a single neuron does

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.600
that no one that no human today understands. For example, exactly how neurons in the human brain

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.320
learns is still a very mysterious process. And it's completely unclear today whether the human brain

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.960
uses an algorithm does anything like back propagation or gradient descent, or if there's some

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.840
fundamentally different learning principle that the human brain uses. So when I think of deep

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.680
learning, I think of it as being very good at learning very flexible functions, very complex

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.680
functions, to learn x to y mappings, to learn input output mappings in supervised learning.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.240
And whereas the is like the brain analogy, maybe that was useful once, I think the field has

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.000
moved to the point where that analogy is breaking down, and I tend not to use that analogy much

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.160
anymore. So that's it for neural networks and their brain. I do think that maybe the field of

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.600
computer vision has taken a bit more inspiration from the human brain than other disciplines that

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.040
also apply deep learning. But I personally use the analogy, you know, to the human brain less

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.520
than I used to. So that's it for this video. You now know how to implement forward prop and

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.080
back prop and gradient descent even for deep neural networks. Best of luck with the program

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.720
exercise. And I look forward to sharing more of these ideas of you in the second course.
